



                     Mach  DOS  Installation  Notes



                      School  of  Computer  Science

                      Carnegie  Mellon  University

       5000  Forbes  Avenue  Pittsburgh,  Pennsylvania  15213



1.  Introduction



This   document   describes   the   procedure   for   building   and
installing   Mach   DOS   emulation   software   (Mdos).       This
software  only  works  with  the  Mach  3.0  kernel.   Mdos  must  be
executed  from  the  console.


   The  Mach  DOS  system  supports  DOS  versions  3.1  to  5.0  and
runs  Windows  3.0  in  real-mode.    We  have  successfully  run
over  100  DOS  applications  ranging  from  business  software
such   as   Lotus   123   and   Microsoft   Word   to   entertainment
software  such  as  Wing  Commander,  Space  Quest  IV,  King's
Quest   V,   Populous,   and   the   Microsoft   Flight   Simulator.
Many  of  these  applications  are  extremely  demanding  in  terms
of  both  graphics  performance  for  VGA  displays  and  sound
generated  by  commercial  monaural  and  stereo  sound  boards.
Note,  only  real-mode  DOS  software  will  execute  successfully
with  the  Mach  DOS  software.    Protected  Mode  DOS  software
such  as  Windows  3.1  currently  will  not  work.


   A  key  feature  of  the  integration  between  the  Mach  DOS
software  and  the  unix  server  is  the  ability  to  access  files
in  the  Unix  File  System  (UFS)  from  within  the  Mach  DOS
environment.    Using  this  ability  you  can  store,  transfer
and  execute  files  from  either  the  normal  DOS  C:  partition
or  the  Unix  File  System  D:  partition.   The  Unix  File  System
features  include  access  to  any  transparent  network  as  well,
including  AFS  and  RFS.



2.  Setup  Requirements



In  order  to  compile  and  run  the  Mdos  emulator  software,  you
must  have  the  following:


   - The  Mdos  release  source  code.


   - A  bootable  version  of  DOS,  either  on  floppy  or  the
     machine's  hard  disk  partition.    WE  DO  NOT  SUPPLY  DOS,
     you  must  purchase  it.    You  must  use  a  version  of  DOS
     between  3.1  and  5.0.


   - You  must  have  a  version  of  Mach  3.0  MK73  or  later  than
     running  on  an  Intel  80386/486  based  machine.


   - A  corresponding  version  of  the  4.3  BSD  Unix  Server  at
     least  as  current  as  UX32.



3.  Building  the  Emulator



To  build  the  Mdos  emulation  software,  you  should  change
directory  to  the  mdos/src  directory.    Make  sure  that  the
mach3  release  area  is  on  your  paths.   To  make  sure,  type:



     setpath  -ib  /usr  /usr/mach3



Note:    if  the  setpath  command  has  the  effect  of  getting
cc  from  /usr/mach3,  you  should  also  set  the  GCC_EXEC_PREFIX
environment  variable  as  follows:



     setenv  GCC_EXEC_PREFIX  /usr/mach3/lib/gcc-



You  can  do  a  wh  cc  to  find  out  what  compiler  you  will  be
using.    If  you  are  using  /bin/cc  you  do  not  want  to  set
GCC_EXEC_PREFIX.


   Once  your  paths  are  setup,  you  should  just  type:



     make



Once  the  makefiles  have  completed,  you  then  enter:



     make  install



This  will  install  the  Mdos  files  in  the  mdos  build  area.   In
addition  to  the  src  directory,  there  will  be  bin,  doc,  lib,
and  man  directories.


4.  Setting  up  Mach  for  DOS



In  order  for  the  Mdos  emulation  software  to  work  with  your
system,  you  must  first  set  up  some  Mach  devices.   Check  that
your  /dev/iopl  and  disk  devices  are  set  correctly.



4.1.   VGA  Access



You  must  make  sure  that  you  have  a  /dev/iopl;  a  character
device  with  major  16  and  minor  0.   It  must  be  read/writeable
by  any  user.   This  is  used  to  access  the  VGA  display  memory
and  the  I/O  ports.   You  can  easily  make  one  of  these  device
files,  by  executing  MAKEDEV  in  the  /dev  directory  as  root  as
follows:



     MAKEDEV  iopl



4.2.   Floppy  Access



Make  sure  that  your  floppy  devices  are  correct.     If  you
use  them  regularly,  they  are  probably  fine.    If  not,  the
simplest  thing  to  do  is  to  remove  what  is  there  and  generate
new  ones.



  1. First,  remove  the  old  ones  as  root,  by  typing:


          rm  -f  /dev/*fd*
          rm  -f  /dev/*floppy*


  2. Second,  change  directories  to  /dev  and  execute  MAKEDEV
     as  root  by  typing:


          MAKEDEV  f<size><unit>


     Where  <size>  is  3  or  5  (3  for  3.5"  disks  and  5  for  5.25"
     disks),  and  <unit>  is  either  0  or  1.   Do  this  for  each
     floppy  device  you  have.   This  creates  /dev/[r]fd<unit>,
     and  /dev/[r]floppy<unit>.


  3. Make  sure  that  you  chmod  them,  so  that  any  user  can  read
     and  write  them:


          chmod  666  /dev/*fd*
          chmod  666  /dev/*floppy*


5.  Setting  up  the  Emulator



The  setup  procedure  entails  creating  a  DOS  boot  floppy,
copying  that  floppy  into  a  UFS  file  image,   and  copying
several  Mach  DOS  configuration  files  onto  it.



5.1.   Making  a  New  DOS  Floppy



First  and  foremost,  you  should  make  a  brand  new,  freshly
formatted  DOS  boot  floppy.   This  new  disk  should  be  written
as  a  high  density  disk.     This  step  is  to  ensure  that
your  boot  floppy  only  has  the  DOS  system  files  (which  are
invisible)  and  a  COMMAND.COM  file  in  the  root  directory.   It
absolutely  should  not  have  an  autoexec.bat,  or  config.sys
file  on  it.     If  you  know  enough  about  DOS  to  create  a
floppy  in  this  manner,  you  will  want  to  skip  the  detailed
instructions.


   Your  DOS  A:  floppy  can  be  either  3.5"  or  5.25";   it
doesn't  matter.



  1. Use  /etc/reboot  to  shut  down  Mach  3.0.


  2. Boot  DOS.


  3. Once  DOS  has  booted  and  you  have  a  prompt,  execute  the
     format  command  as  follows:


          format  a:   /s


     This  will  ask  you  to  place  the  floppy  to  be  formatted
     into  the  drive.     Place  the  new  disk  into  the  drive,
     close  the  door,  and  press  a  key.



   When  the  format  command  has  finished,  remove  your  new  DOS
boot  floppy  from  the  A:  drive.   This  newly  formatted  floppy
will  be  referred  to  as  the  DOS  boot  floppy  in  the  rest  of
this  document.


   Do  not  put  either  an  autoexec.bat,  or  a  config.sys  file
on  this  floppy.   These  will  be  added  to  your  floppy  in  the
next  stage.


5.2.   Copying  DOS  Boot  Floppy  into  UFS



You  must  have  a  copy  of  the  DOS  boot  code  and  the  actual  DOS
operating  system  available  to  Mach  DOS.  This  can  be  either  a
floppy  disk  in  the  disk  drive,  a  DOS  hard  disk  partition,
or  it  can  be  Unix  File  System  image  of  a  DOS  boot  floppy.
The  latter  is  the  usual  choice  and  is  discussed  in  the
installation  procedures.   For  information  on  setting  up  your
DOS  hard  disk  partition  for  Mach  DOS,  see  the  appendices.


   By  default,  the  Mach  DOS  software  looks  for  a  file  called
ms.dos  at  invocation.   It  looks  for  this  file  on  your  LPATH
or  in  your  HOME/lib  directory.     To  generate  this  file,
follow  these  instructions:



  1. Remove  the  newly  formatted  DOS  boot  floppy  from  the
     floppy  disk  drive.


  2. Simultaneously  press  the  Control,  Alt,  and  Delete  keys.
     This  reboots  your  machine  when  running  DOS.


  3. Bring  Mach  3.0  up  and  log  in.


  4. Change   directory   to   the   lib   directory   in   your   home
     directory.


  5. Insert  the  new  DOS  boot  floppy  in  the  A:  drive  and  close
     the  door.


  6. Execute  the  following  command:


          cp  /dev/rfloppy  ms.dos


     This  will  take  a  couple  of  minutes.



5.3.   Copying  Special  Files  onto  Floppy  Image



The  file  dos.floppy,  included  in  your  release,  is  an  image
of  a  DOS  floppy  that  contains  several  important  Mach  DOS
files.     In  order  for  the  Mach  DOS  software  to  execute
correctly,  and  to  enable  access  to  the  Unix  File  System,
you  must  copy  the  files  from  this  floppy  image  onto  your
DOS  boot  floppy.    These  files  must  be  present  on  any  DOS
media  you  wish  to  boot  from  including  your  DOS  hard  disk
partition.    For  further  information  on  the  files  contained
in  the  dos.floppy  image,  refer  to  the  appendices.


   In  order  to  copy  the  files  from  the  dos.floppy  file  to
your  ms.dos  file,  follow  these  instructions:


  1. Change  directory  to  the  mdos  build  area.


  2. Start  the  Mach  DOS  software  by  executing  the  following
     instruction:


          mdos  -f  -db  dos.floppy


     This  will  begin  Mdos  execution.


  3. Once  the  A>  prompt  is  displayed  enter:


          b:cpbatch


     And  hit  return.    This  will  copy  the  setup  files  onto
     your  ms.dos  floppy  image.


  4. Simultaneously  press  the  Control,  Alt,  and  Delete  keys.
     This  exits  Mach  DOS.



   The  installation  of  Mach  DOS  is  now  complete.    You  only
need  to  type  mdos  to  begin  execution  of  Mach  DOS.  If  you
plan   to   use   a   DOS   hard   disk   partition   with   Mach   DOS,
make  sure  that  the  autoexec.bat  and  config.sys  files  don't
include  any  drivers  that  adversely  effect  execution (see appendix D).



6.  Executing  Mdos



To  run  the  Mach  Dos  Emulator  you  only  need  to  type  "mdos"
and  hit  enter.


   For  more  information,  refer  to  the  man  page  in  the  man
directory in  the  mdos  release  area.

   Have  fun.




7.  Setting  up  to  use  a  DOS  Hard  Disk  Partition



If  you  didn't  leave  room  on  your  machine's  hard  disk  for  DOS
and clobbered  all  of  the  hard  disk's  space,  your  machine
doesn't have  a  DOS  hard  disk  partition  and  you  should  skip
this  part  of  the  installation.   If  you  left  room  during  the
Mach  installation  and  have  a  DOS  partition  on  your  machine's
hard  disk,  you  can  use  it  with  the  Mdos  software.


   In  order  for  the  Mdos  emulator  to  access  the  DOS  hard
disk  partition,  you  must  create  an  entry  in  the  vtoc  (Volume
Table  of  Contents)  which  points  to  the  correct  area  on  the
disk.   To  do  this,  you  must  use  the  program  diskutil  which
lives  in  the  /etc  directory.   You  should  read  the  man  page
for  diskutil  and  become  familiar  with  its  operation.   Become
root  and  proceed  as  follows:



  1. Execute  the  /etc/diskutil  program  as  follows:


          /etc/diskutil  vtoc


     Examine  the  vtoc  printout,  and  find  an  empty  partition
     entry  (ie.   h:   MT).   Typically  I  use  the  empty  partition
     "h".


  2. Execute  the  /etc/diskutil  program  again:


          /etc/diskutil  fdisk


     Examine  the  fdisk  printout.    Determine  which  partition
     is  the  DOS  partition  (0-4).   The  DOS  partition  will  have
     a  systid  of  either  1,  4,  or  6  (the  Mach  systid  number  is
     99).


  3. After  determining  the  DOS  partition  number  from  the
     fdisk  output,  and  have  chosen  a  vtoc  partition,  execute
     the  /etc/diskutil  program  again:


          diskutil    dos    -<dos    partition    number>    -
          <MT  vtoc  partition>


     The  diskutil  program  will  make  the  correct  entry  into
     the   vtoc   for   you   and   will   ask   if   it   should   save
     the   changes   permanently.       Unless   you   have   strong
     reservations,  say  yes.


  4. Change   the   permissions   so   that   the   partition   is
     read/writeable  by  everyone:


          chmod  666  /dev/*hd0<MT  vtoc  partition>


  5. Make   a   soft   link   in   the   /dev   directory   from   the
     raw  partition  file  to  the  name  dosdisk  in  the  /dev
     directory.


          ln  -s  /dev/rhd0<MT  vtoc  partition>  /dev/dosdisk



   That's  it,  your  DOS  hard  disk  partition  is  ready  for  use
with  the  Mdos  emulation  software.


Appendices



A  Using  Low  Density  DOS  disks



You  must  set  up  a  special  low  density  device  file  in  /dev  if
you  are  going  to  use  low  density  disks  (by  default  they  are
set  to  high  density  devices).



B  The  dos.floppy  file



This  file  is  an  image  of  a  real  DOS  formatted  5.25"  inch
floppy  disk.   It  contains  the  cpbatch.bat  batch  file  that  is
run  from  Mach  DOS  during  the  installation  procedure.


   Included  on  this  "floppy"  are  the  following  files:



   - autoexec.bat  This  file  sets  up  your  path,  and  executes
     the  mfsini.exe  file.


   - config.sys  This  file  contains  the  machfs.sys  device  in
     it.


   - machfs.sys  This  file  is  the  driver  that  install  the  UFS
     as  a  DOS  network  drive  when  Mdos  is  executed.


   - mfsini.exe  This  file  is  used  in  conjunction  with  the
     machfs.sys  file,   to  initialize  the  UFS  access  under
     Mdos.


   - cpbatch.bat  This  file  is  used  to  copy  the  files  from
     dos.floppy  onto  your  DOS  boot  floppy  image.



C  Useful  DOS  programs



It  will  be  handy  to  have  a  copy  of  the  most  useful  DOS
programs  on  your  DOS  boot  floppy's  UFS  file  image.     I
suggest  that  you  make  a  directory  named  DOS  on  your  DOS  A:
drive  when  executing  the  Mdos  monitor  using  the  following
command:



     mkdir  a:\dos



   Next,   copy   all   of   the   basic   DOS   programs   into   this
directory.      These   programs   include   the   EDLIN.EXE,   and
DEBUG.EXE  commands.    It  will  depend  on  where  you  get  these
from  as  to  what  your  command  line  will  look  like.     An
example  would  be:



     copy  c:\dos\*.*  a:\dos



   This  step  is  optional  for  installation,   but  you  will
probably  want  these  files  sooner  or  later.    Just  make  sure
that  you  don't  copy  a  autoexec.bat  or  config.sys  file  onto
your  ms.dos  file.



D  Special  DOS  Files



D1.   Your  config.sys  file



The  DOS  config.sys  file  resides  in  the  root  directory  of
your   DOS   file   systems,   and   instructs   DOS   which   device
drivers  to  load  into  your  system  at  boot  time.    You  can
only  load  device  drivers  at  boot  time  (unless  you  know  DOS
internals).    When  you  execute  the  Mdos  emulation  software,
it  is  important  which  device  files  you  use,  and  which  ones
you  absolutely  should  NOT  use.    Here  are  lists  of  drivers
which  can  and  cannot  be  put  in  your  config.sys  file:



   - Drivers  you  MUST  include:


      --  MACHFS.SYS  You  must  include  this  driver  if  you  plan
          on  using  the  UFS  as  a  DOS  drive.


   - Drivers  you  CAN  include:


      --  FILES=XX

      --  BUFFERS=XX

      --  DOS=HIGH

      --  Ramdisks

      --  Popupkeys


   - Drivers  you  CANNOT  include:


      --  Mouse  drivers  The  Mdos  software  has  a  built-in  mouse
          driver.    The  mouse  driver  you  put  in  the  config.sys
          file  will  fail  and/or  probably  hang.   This  is  not  a
          bug,  just  don't  do  it  (we  do  the  work  for  you).

      --  EMS  drivers  The  Mdos  software  also  has  a  built-in
          EMS  driver.   Do  not  install  your  own.

      --  XMS  drivers  The  Mdos  software  has  a  built-in  XMS
          driver.   Do  not  install  your  own.


   - Drivers  you  SHOULDN'T  include:


      --  SmartDrives   The   Mdos   software   doesn't   chain   Int
          0x13's  (ie.     your  driver  will  just  sit  there  and
          never  be  called).   Don't  use  them.



D2.   Your  autoexec.bat



The  DOS  autoexec.bat  file  resides  in  the  root  directories  of
your  DOS  file  systems  just  like  the  config.sys  file.    The
autoexec.bat  batch  file  is  a  batch  file  which  is  executed
at  DOS  boot  time,  after  the  devices  have  been  loaded  from
the  config.sys  file.     Under  the  Mdos  emulation  software,
like  the  config.sys  file,  the  autoexec.bat  file  must  contain
certain  commands.    Unlike  the  config.sys  file,  there  isn't
anything  which  should  be  kept  out.   The  only  thing  which  you
must  put  in  your  autoexec.bat  file  is  the  line:



     mfsini.exe



This  line  should  execute  the  program  mfsini.exe  which  is
supplied  in  the  dos.floppy  file.



D3.   Modifying   config.sys   and   autoexec.bat   on   the   C:
      partition



In  next  release.



E  Known  "Bugs"



There   are   many   things   that   work   well   under   the   Mdos
software,  and  many  things  that  don't  work  well.   This  is  a
short  list  of  "features"  that  are  known  to  exist,  and  some
of  their  corresponding  "bugs":



   - CGA  based  programs  may  have  problems.     Specifically,
     public  domain  programs  that  need  Mouse  driver  based
     pointers  in  CGA  mode  won't  work.    If  you  want  to  get
     down  and  dirty  with  the  code,  look  in  bios_mouse.c.


   - Keyboard  bugs.    I've  fixed  all  the  keyboard  bugs  I've
     run  across,  but  I've  heard  "rumors"  of  some  other  ones
     existing.    If  you  come  across  a  particular  one,  send
     some  mail  about  it.


   - The  Mouse  under  Windows  only  works  when  the  Logitech
     serial  mouse  driver  is  installed  with  the  Window's  setup
     program.   I  plan  on  adding  more  mouse  support  code  that
     will  autodetect  your  mouse  type,  and  emulate  its  driver.
     To  do  this,  look  in  bios_com.c  for  the  logitech  mouse
     driver  emulation  code.


   - Sound  problems.     In  order  for  some  DOS  programs  to
     get  the  sound  they  require  from  the  PC  speaker,  they
     use  timer  interrupts  of  a  granularity  impossible  to
     reproduce  with  any  accuracy  under  Mach.   I  suggest  that
     if  this  type  of  sound  is  important  to  you,  that  you  buy
     one  of  the  many  commercial  sound  cards  available  for
     the  PC.  You  can  try  and  tweak  the  sound  code  that's
     available.   Look  at  the  sources  in:   bios_kbd.c,  and  the
     kernel's  i386/trap.c.


   - DOS  uses  all  the  cycles  it  can  get.   When  DOS  is  waiting
     for  input,  it  polls  the  keyboard.    Therefore  you  will
     see  a  very  high  load  whenever  DOS  is  using  the  machine.
     This  can  be  fixed.    One  of  several  possible  solutions
     to  the  problem,  is  to  catch  the  DOS  exec  routine,  and
     watch  for  COMMAND.COM.  When  running  this  shell,  block  on
     keyboard  input  in  the  bios_kbd.c  code.


   - Only   files   with   legal   DOS   file   system   names   are
     recognized  in  the  UFS.  This  means  that  names  that  have
     more  that  one  period  (dot)  in  them,  have  dollar  signs  or
     commas  in  them,  or  that  are  longer  than  eight  letters
     before  the  period,  or  longer  than  three  letters  after
     the  period  are  not  recognized.     This  can  be  fixed,
     but  the  DOS  DIR  command  will  be  very  confused.    Major
     revision  to  the  code  in  dos_disk.c,  and  dos_fs.c  will  be
     needed  to  change  this.


   - The  DOS  DIR  command  is  slow  under  the  UFS  (especially
     AFS).  This  is  because  an  lstat  must  be  done  on  each
     file  in  the  directory.     The  lstat  is  needed  to  fill
     out  all  of  the  fields  in  the  DOS  DIR  command.    This
     could  be  made  to  work  much  faster,  possibly  by  utilizing
     knowledge  of  a  directory  inode  (hack).     Code  changes
     would  be  to  dos_disk.c,  and  dos_fs.c.


   - You  cannot  FORMAT  disks  with  the  Mach  DOS  software.
     This  applies  to  both  the  DOS  hard  disk  partition  and
     floppy  disks.    The  reason  for  this,  is  that  the  BIOS
     disk  device  drivers  do  not  work  under  Mach  DOS  and  the
     BIOS  format  calls  are  not  emulated  by  the  Mach  DOS
     software.   Currently,  you  must  do  all  DOS  formats  under
     native  DOS.



   I'm  sure  there  are  other  things  I  have  forgotten  to  write
down  that  you  should  know.   If  you  come  across  anything  you
think  should  be  added  to  this  list,  or  have  any  problems,
please  feel  free  to  send  mail  and  let  us  know.    As  these
problems  are  addressed,  this  list  will  grow  and  change,
helping  future  Mdos  users.
